{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 17,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.046,
            0.364,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.087,
            0.848,
            0.127
          ],
          "content": "Use your understanding of such relationships in multiplication to answer the questions above."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.149,
            0.699,
            0.174
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "8.3 Some Problems Involving Fractions"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.189,
            0.846,
            0.243
          ],
          "content": "？ **Example 3:** Leena made 5 cups of tea. She used $\\frac { 1 } { 4 }$ litre of milk for this. How much milk is there in each cup of tea?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.191,
            0.27,
            0.793,
            0.395
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_17_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.418,
            0.848,
            0.474
          ],
          "content": "Leena used $\\frac { 1 } { 4 }$ litres of milk in 5 cups of tea. So, in 1 cup of tea the volume of milk should be:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.469,
            0.483,
            0.528,
            0.519
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{1}{4} \\div 5.",
          "image_source": "images/page_17_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.521,
            0.548,
            0.543
          ],
          "content": "Writing this as multiplication, we have:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.388,
            0.554,
            0.608,
            0.588
          ],
          "content": "5 \\times ( \\mathrm{mink} \\mathrm{per} \\mathrm{cap} ) = \\frac{1}{4}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_17_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.591,
            0.807,
            0.612
          ],
          "content": "We perform the division as follows as per Brahmagupta’s method:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.614,
            0.512,
            0.646
          ],
          "content": "The reciprocal of 5 (the divisor) is $\\frac{1}{5},$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.643,
            0.698,
            0.674
          ],
          "content": "Multiplying this reciprocal by the dividend $( \\frac { 1 } { 4 } ) ,$ , we get"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.446,
            0.68,
            0.552,
            0.717
          ],
          "content": "\\frac { 1 } { 5 } \\times \\frac { 1 } { 4 } = \\frac { 1 } { 2 0 } .",
          "image_source": "images/page_17_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.716,
            0.559,
            0.751
          ],
          "content": "So, each cup of tea has $\\frac { 1 } { 2 0 }$ litre of milk."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.763,
            0.848,
            0.822
          ],
          "content": "**Example 4:** Some of the oldest examples of working with non-unit fractions occur in humanity’s oldest geometry texts, the Śhulbasūtra. Here is an example from Baudhāyana’s Śhulbasūtra (c. 800 BCE)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.828,
            0.848,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": "Cover an area of 7 $\\frac { 1 } { 2 }$ square units with square bricks each of whose sides is $\\frac { 1 } { 5 }$ units."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.938,
            0.194,
            0.955
          ],
          "content": "190"
        },
        {
